{"externally_confirmed_facts":["`javax.crypto.SecretKeyFactory` is a JCA factory for secret keys. Oracle documents that it converts between opaque `SecretKey` objects and transparent key specifications: `generateSecret(KeySpec)` generates a secret key from a key specification and `getKeySpec` obtains a specification for a key. Supported `KeySpec` types are provider-specific.","The reported value `PBKDF2WithHmacSHA1` refers to the `algorithm` argument of `SecretKeyFactory.getInstance(algorithm)` or `SecretKeyFactory.getInstance(algorithm, provider)`. It does not, from the cited CRYSL rule, refer to a `PBEKeySpec` password, salt, iteration count, requested key length, ciphertext format, or the `generateSecret` call itself.","Oracle documents that `SecretKeyFactory.getInstance` requests an implementation of the named secret-key algorithm from registered providers. The overloads with a provider name or provider object restrict selection to that provider; an unavailable algorithm causes `NoSuchAlgorithmException`. Oracle's provider guidance says general-purpose applications normally should not pin a provider, because doing so can reduce portability and bypass a higher-priority implementation.","Oracle's standard-name specification defines `PBKDF2With<prf>` as PBKDF2 using the named PRF and gives `PBKDF2WithHmacSHA256` as an example. It defines `PBEWith<prf>And<encryption>` as a PKCS #5 password-based-encryption factory name. Standard names are documented as not case-sensitive.","For password-based usage, `PBEKeySpec` documents use of `char[]`, rather than `String`, so password material can be overwritten, and provides `clearPassword()`. Its constructors with salt reject null or empty salt and non-positive iteration counts.","NIST SP 800-132, for PBKDF2 in storage applications, specifies an approved random-generated salt portion of at least 128 bits, an iteration count as large as practical for the application, a derived-key length of at least 112 bits, and PBKDF2 using HMAC with an approved hash function. This document does not establish an application-specific policy or the CRYSL rule's policy rationale.","The official `SecretKeyFactory` API documentation does not prescribe a replacement of `PBKDF2WithHmacSHA1` with any particular one of the rule's listed names, and it does not define the CRYSL allowlist as a Java-platform safety policy. Oracle's current SunJCE provider documentation lists `PBKDF2WithHmacSHA1` as a supported `SecretKeyFactory` algorithm. Thus, support by a provider and acceptance by this specific rule are distinct documented matters.","The directly documented guidance relevant to use otherwise is to use the documented standard algorithm name, rely on provider documentation for supported algorithms and `KeySpec` forms, and—where portability is wanted in a general-purpose application—normally use the no-provider `getInstance` form. Neither the `SecretKeyFactory` API nor its provider documentation supplies a general remediation or migration prescription from one PBKDF2 PRF to another.","`SecretKeyFactory` specifies no persisted-data format, wire format, serialized representation, or data-migration mechanism. Provider support for particular key specifications and algorithms is provider-dependent.","RFC 8018 specifies that PKCS #5 PBKDF2 parameters contain salt, iteration count, optional key length, and a PRF `AlgorithmIdentifier`; if the PRF is omitted, its default is HMAC-SHA-1. It specifies PBES2 parameters as a key-derivation-function identifier plus an encryption-scheme identifier. Therefore, in an RFC 8018 PBKDF2/PBES2 encoding, the PRF/KDF selection is protocol-relevant metadata. RFC 8018 does not provide a Java-specific general migration path between PRFs.","Oracle documents that every Java implementation must support `DESede` for `SecretKeyFactory`, while support for other algorithms must be verified against the applicable release and provider documentation. Oracle's Java SE 7 SunJCE documentation lists `PBKDF2WithHmacSHA1` but not the SHA-224/256/384/512 PBKDF2 variants; its JDK 8 SunJCE documentation lists SHA-1 and SHA-224/256/384/512 PBKDF2 variants; current JDK 25 SunJCE documentation lists those variants and additional SHA-512 truncated variants. These are documented Oracle-provider/version facts, not guarantees for every JVM, update level, or third-party provider.","The cited CRYSL file declares `SPEC javax.crypto.SecretKeyFactory`; its receiver/API type is therefore `javax.crypto.SecretKeyFactory`. Its matching event declarations, quoted verbatim, are:
```
EVENTS
    g1: getInstance(algorithm);
    g2: getInstance(algorithm, _);
    Get := g1 | g2;

    gS: key = generateSecret(keySpec);
    tK: key = translateKey(otherKey);
    Gen := gS | tK;
```
Thus, as declared in the rule, it covers `getInstance` with one or two arguments, `generateSecret` with one `keySpec` argument and an assigned result, and `translateKey` with one `otherKey` argument and an assigned result. The two-argument `getInstance` event leaves its second argument as `_`; the rule supplies no further constraint for that argument.","The rule's ordering and predicates, quoted verbatim, are:
```
ORDER
    Get, Gen
CONSTRAINTS
    algorithm in {"PBKDF2WithHmacSHA512", "PBKDF2WithHmacSHA384", "PBKDF2WithHmacSHA256", "PBKDF2WithHmacSHA224",
                "PBEWithHmacSHA512AndAES_128","PBEWithHmacSHA384AndAES_128", "PBEWithHmacSHA384AndAES_128",
                "PBEWithHmacSHA224AndAES_128", "PBEWithHmacSHA256AndAES_128","PBEWithHmacSHA224AndAES_256",
                "PBEWithHmacSHA256AndAES_256", "PBEWithHmacSHA384AndAES_256", "PBEWithHmacSHA512AndAES_256"};

REQUIRES
    speccedKey[keySpec, _];

ENSURES
    generatedKey[key, algorithm];
```
The evaluated `CONSTRAINTS` condition is membership of `algorithm` in that literal set. The rule has the duplicated literal `PBEWithHmacSHA384AndAES_128`. Its `REQUIRES` condition is applied to `keySpec`, and its `ENSURES` clause records `generatedKey[key, algorithm]`.","The rule text itself does not declare a name-only matcher: it scopes the specification to `javax.crypto.SecretKeyFactory`. Therefore, under the rule as written, a method merely sharing one of these names is not declared as a match. The CRYSL source does not document analyzer implementation details for unresolved dispatch or type resolution, so it cannot independently establish operational behavior in such cases."],"sources":[{"relevance":"Official Java API documentation for the governed type, factory selection, exceptions, key generation/specification conversion, provider dependence, and baseline required support.","limitations":"Does not prescribe a particular replacement for `PBKDF2WithHmacSHA1`; does not define persistence, wire-format, or migration behavior.","claim":"Defines `SecretKeyFactory` semantics and the `getInstance`, `generateSecret`, and `getKeySpec` APIs.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/crypto/SecretKeyFactory.html"},{"relevance":"Official Java standard-name documentation for PBKDF2 and PBE naming and case treatment.","limitations":"Defines standard naming, not a complete algorithm-selection policy or migration procedure.","claim":"Defines `PBKDF2With<prf>` and `PBEWith<prf>And<encryption>` standard-name forms and says standard names are not case-sensitive.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/specs/security/standard-names.html"},{"relevance":"Official API documentation for password key-spec handling.","limitations":"Does not set an application-specific PBKDF2 work factor, salt policy beyond constructor validity checks, or algorithm migration process.","claim":"Documents `char[]` password handling, `clearPassword()`, and validation of salt and iteration-count constructor arguments.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/crypto/spec/PBEKeySpec.html"},{"relevance":"Official Oracle provider documentation for current SunJCE algorithm availability and provider-selection guidance.","limitations":"Describes Oracle's documented provider configuration, not every Java runtime or third-party provider.","claim":"Lists `PBKDF2WithHmacSHA1` and SHA-2 PBKDF2 variants as current SunJCE `SecretKeyFactory` algorithms and advises general-purpose applications normally not to pin providers.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/security/oracle-providers.html"},{"relevance":"Official historical Oracle provider documentation relevant to version support.","limitations":"Only establishes the documented Java SE 7 SunJCE list, not later updates or non-Oracle distributions/providers.","claim":"Lists `PBKDF2WithHmacSHA1` in the Java SE 7 SunJCE provider documentation.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/7/docs/technotes/guides/security/SunProviders.html"},{"relevance":"Official historical Oracle provider documentation relevant to version support.","limitations":"Only establishes the documented JDK 8 SunJCE list, not every JDK 8 update or other providers.","claim":"Lists `PBKDF2WithHmacSHA1`, `PBKDF2WithHmacSHA224`, `PBKDF2WithHmacSHA256`, `PBKDF2WithHmacSHA384`, and `PBKDF2WithHmacSHA512` for SunJCE.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/technotes/guides/security/SunProviders.html"},{"relevance":"Official NIST guidance for PBKDF2 parameters in storage applications.","limitations":"Published in 2010 and scoped to storage applications; it does not identify the intended policy behind the supplied detector rule or prescribe an application-specific transition.","claim":"Specifies approved-random salt, iteration-count, derived-key-length, and approved-HMAC guidance for the document's PBKDF2 storage-application scope.","authority":"official","url":"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/SP/nistspecialpublication800-132.pdf"},{"relevance":"Primary protocol specification for standardized PKCS #5 PBKDF2 and PBES2 parameter encodings.","limitations":"Does not identify any local data format and does not specify a Java-specific migration procedure.","claim":"Defines `PBKDF2-params`, including PRF treatment and default HMAC-SHA-1 when absent, and PBES2 parameter structure.","authority":"primary","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8018.html"},{"relevance":"Primary source for the detector rule's receiver type, matched event declarations, order, constraints, requirements, and ensured predicate.","limitations":"Does not explain the policy rationale; does not define `speccedKey`; and does not document analyzer behavior for aliases, case normalization, unresolved dispatch, exceptions, reachability, or type-resolution failures.","claim":"Declares `SPEC javax.crypto.SecretKeyFactory`, event patterns for `getInstance`, `generateSecret`, and `translateKey`, the literal algorithm allowlist, and the `speccedKey`/`generatedKey` predicates.","authority":"primary","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/SecretKeyFactory.crysl"}],"queries_performed":["Reviewed the supplied primary CRYSL source for `SecretKeyFactory.crysl`: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/SecretKeyFactory.crysl","Reviewed Oracle Java SE 25 `javax.crypto.SecretKeyFactory` API documentation.","Reviewed Oracle Java standard algorithm-name documentation and `PBEKeySpec` API documentation.","Reviewed Oracle SunJCE provider documentation for Java SE 7, JDK 8, and JDK 25 availability statements.","Reviewed NIST SP 800-132 and RFC 8018 for documented PBKDF2 parameter and encoding semantics."],"externally_unresolved_questions":["The CRYSL file does not state why its specific allowlist was selected or whether it is intended as a universal security policy.","The CRYSL file references `speccedKey[keySpec, _]` but does not define that predicate or identify its producer rule.","The CRYSL source alone does not specify detector behavior for aliases, algorithm-name capitalization/normalization, exception paths, infeasible paths, provider availability, unresolved dispatch, or type-resolution failures.","No supplied evidence identifies the actual runtime Java version, installed provider(s), provider order, or whether a proposed algorithm name is available in the applicable environment.","No supplied evidence identifies whether local persisted data or an interoperable wire protocol uses RFC 8018 PBKDF2/PBES2 encoding, records KDF/PRF metadata, or requires compatibility with an existing algorithm.","Neither the `SecretKeyFactory` API documentation nor the cited protocol and provider documents provide a general migration path between PBKDF2 PRFs."]}
